is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce any network-side freezes or loss on the path pc_0_0 -> leaf_router_0_0 -> spine -> leaf_router_0_1 -> pc_0_1. End-to-end reachability and latency were consistently healthy: 200 back-to-back pings from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 showed 0% loss with 0.55–1.94 ms RTT (avg ~1.15 ms), and large-MTU probes up to 1472 bytes with DF set succeeded (no PMTU blackholing). A 3-second iperf run delivered 129–152 Mbps with 0 retransmits, indicating stable TCP forwarding. Traceroute was steady over 4 hops (10.0.0.1 → 172.16.0.4 → 172.16.0.7 → 10.0.1.2). 

Fabric control-plane and forwarding state are normal: all BGP sessions across leaves, spines, and super-spine are Established and exchanging expected prefixes; routing tables on all routers select the correct next hops for 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24. Interface health is clean: all links report 10G/full, link detected yes; no errors/drops on host or router interfaces; tc fq_codel queues show zero drops/overlimits and empty backlogs.

Mechanistically, if the network were intermittently flapping you would see loss, spikes far beyond ~2 ms, BGP neighbor resets, interface errors, or queue drops—none were observed. Localization therefore points to no faulty device in the data center fabric. Root cause: no network fault was present during investigation; earlier freezes were not reproducible and are likely outside the fabric or transient host/application conditions. 

Corrective fix: none required on the fabric. If the issue recurs, please capture timestamps and we can run longer iperf/smokeping and packet captures; also check host CPU/disk contention and NIC driver state during file copies.
=== END ===